WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:07.810
You know, I often get told, “Thoughty2,
you’re so intelligent!” To which I always

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:13.589
reply,, “Thanks Mum, but really, what is
intelligence anyway?”

00:00:13.589 --> 00:00:17.940
Which sounds kind of profound, even if I do
say so myself.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:22.260
Turns out, though, it’s a question that’s
far from straightforward to answer. We all

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:27.450
seem to have an internal intelligence radar,
that part of us that unconsciously decides,

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:32.410
“She’s a genius” or “He probably can’t
wipe his own arse.”

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:38.190
But that level of subjectivity isn’t useful
when trying to establish a standard definition

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:44.470
of intelligence. You might see Albert Einstein
as your intellectual hero, while I might regard

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:48.620
Ronald McDonald as the greatest philosopher
of our times.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:50.120
Which I don’t. Anymore.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.790
Unsurprisingly, I’m not the first person
to spot this problem. People have been trying

00:00:54.790 --> 00:01:00.489
to define intelligence for centuries, but
so far, nobody has come up with a definition

00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:07.110
that everyone can agree on. And this is a
hot topic of debate, the kind that gets psychologists

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:12.580
foaming at the mouth and shouting stuff about
Oedipus. It’s almost as controversial as

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.760
the testing of intelligence, but more on that
a bit later.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.990
The Oxford Dictionary defines intelligence
as “The ability to acquire and apply knowledge

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:27.940
and skills.” Sounds pretty good, in my opinion.
If it were up to me, we’d all shake hands

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:32.430
and break for an early lunch, but there’s
no pleasing some people.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:37.330
Over the years, multiple social scientists
have offered competing definitions of intelligence.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:42.659
It’s been described as , “The ability
to deal with cognitive complexity.” by some

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:48.840
and “Goal-directed adaptive behaviour”
by others. Physicist and computer scientist

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:54.020
Alex Wissner-Gross even defined intelligence
through the power of mathematics using this

00:01:54.020 --> 00:02:00.740
rather nifty equation, where F is the force
of intelligence, T is the strength to maintain

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:07.770
future actions, and S is the diversity of
future options over the time horizon Tau.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:13.000
Unfortunately, you have to be a genius to
actually understand the equation in the first

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.000
place.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.480
Some brave souls have tried to build a standard
view of intelligence. In 1994, professor of

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.330
educational psychology Linda Gottfredson prepared
a statement for the Wall Street Journal claiming

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:33.840
to represent the mainstream science on intelligence.
She invited 131 experts to sign the statement,

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.629
including specialists in anthropology, behaviour
genetics, mental retardation, neuropsychology,

00:02:38.629 --> 00:02:45.250
sociology, and a bunch of other jobs that
sound interesting at dinner parties.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:50.840
Fifty-two university professors signed the
statement, which laid out 25 different conclusions

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:56.260
about intelligence. Sounds convincing, but
the declaration was later criticised by several

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:01.410
experts and psychologists, not least because
it supported the idea that intelligence is

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:08.290
genetically determined by race and included
such pearls of racist wisdom as: ‘Genetically

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:13.620
caused differences in intelligence are not
necessarily irremediable’.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:18.280
These days, there are so many different definitions
for intelligence that when researchers Shane

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:24.349
Legg and Marcus Hutter tried to catalogue
all of them, they gave up counting at 70,

00:03:24.349 --> 00:03:27.780
but said there were many more.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.810
You might be wondering why it’s so important
to define intelligence. It’s not like you

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:36.500
can put it on a sandwich, so who cares if
we disagree about what it is?

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:41.600
Well, one reason there’s an argument about
this is that people just like to be right.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.810
And another is that recently, people have
become sticky about defining intelligence

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:53.360
because they want to be able to better define
artificial intelligence. At some point, possibly

00:03:53.360 --> 00:04:00.049
in the not-too-distant future, someone will
create a machine with true artificial intelligence.

00:04:00.049 --> 00:04:05.330
But, to make sure they deserve all the fame,
fortune, and cyberporn that comes with being

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:11.500
the world’s most famous nerd, the machine’s
intelligence will need to be verified; it

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:14.140
will need to pass a test.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:19.979
Since 1950, the accepted standard has been
the Turing Test, suggested by the father of

00:04:19.979 --> 00:04:27.160
computer science himself, Alan Turing. Turing’s
simple idea, which he called the Imitation

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:33.820
Game, was that a machine could be considered
intelligent if someone talking to it was unable

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:39.319
to work out whether they were communicating
with a human or a computer.

00:04:39.319 --> 00:04:47.290
In 2014 it was claimed the Turing Test had
been passed by a computer program called Eugene

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:53.339
Goostman, which simulates a 13-year-old Ukrainian
boy. The claim has been disputed, though,

00:04:53.339 --> 00:04:58.639
which doesn’t surprise me. I once convinced
my mate Dave that I was a 16-year-old schoolgirl

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:04.759
named Gretchen, and no-one gave me a prize.
Anyway, when theoretical computer scientist

00:05:04.759 --> 00:05:11.249
Scott Aaronson asked Eugene how many legs
a camel has, Eugene answered: ‘Something

00:05:11.249 --> 00:05:17.659
between 2 and 4. Maybe, three?’ So at this
point my best guess is that the 30% of judges

00:05:17.659 --> 00:05:20.349
he fooled were all just high.

00:05:20.349 --> 00:05:25.719
So the Turing Test probably still stands,
but some say it won’t be complex enough

00:05:25.719 --> 00:05:35.999
to test full AI in the future. And if we’re
going to develop a new test, we’ll first

00:05:35.999 --> 00:05:40.060
need to agree on what counts as intelligence.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:43.879
Another reason it’s important to define
intelligence is that it has consistently been

00:05:43.879 --> 00:05:49.849
used to make judgements about who should get
what job, who should play what role in society,

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:54.029
and even who should live and who should die.

00:05:54.029 --> 00:05:59.319
It began in the late nineteenth century with
Charles Darwin’s cousin, Francis Galton.

00:05:59.319 --> 00:06:03.499
If you’ve seen my video on the wisdom of
crowds you’ll know that Galton had a particular

00:06:03.499 --> 00:06:09.379
fondness for oxen, and that he was interested
in applying evolutionary theory to humans

00:06:09.379 --> 00:06:15.710
in, shall we say ‘interesting’ ways. He
concluded that a person’s abilities were

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:22.240
inherited, and theorised that intelligence
could be predicted by examining an individual’s

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:29.300
physical traits, like their reflexes, muscle
grip, or head size. Of course, by those standards

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:35.190
Andre the Giant would be a genius, so it’s
no shock Galton found no correlation in the

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:36.190
end.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:42.770
He did, however, become certain that some
humans are genetically superior to others,

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:50.899
and that desirable and undesirable genetic
traits could and should be controlled in humans

00:06:50.899 --> 00:06:56.669
through selective breeding. Basically, the
human race would be better off if we got rid

00:06:56.669 --> 00:07:01.949
of the messy bits. This didn’t go down well
with everyone, especially not with the less

00:07:01.949 --> 00:07:07.199
desirables, but that didn’t bother Galton.
In his own words, "There exists a sentiment,

00:07:07.199 --> 00:07:13.399
for the most part quite unreasonable, against
the gradual extinction of an inferior race."

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:19.050
The idea of selective breeding in humans wasn’t
new, but Galton was the one to give it a catchy

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:24.679
name - eugenics - and it wouldn’t be long
before it became closely tied to intelligence

00:07:24.679 --> 00:07:25.679
testing.

00:07:25.679 --> 00:07:32.259
At the turn of the 20th-century, British psychologist
Charles Spearman was grappling with the question

00:07:32.259 --> 00:07:38.629
of whether intelligence is one thing or a
bunch of different talents and capabilities.

00:07:38.629 --> 00:07:45.560
In 1904, he declared the existence of a general
human intelligence he called the G-factor

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:51.560
- something which, to this day, women claim
men are unable to find.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:56.479
According to Spearman, people may have a range
of natural talents, such as their skill with

00:07:56.479 --> 00:08:03.279
words, numbers, or spatial comprehension,
but below all of these capabilities is a single

00:08:03.279 --> 00:08:07.550
intelligence that differs from one person
to another.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:12.259
He came to this conclusion through a process
called factor analysis, which uses various

00:08:12.259 --> 00:08:18.029
tests to identify correlations between different
variables. Spearman found that people who

00:08:18.029 --> 00:08:23.919
did well in one area of cognitive testing
often did well in other areas of testing too.

00:08:23.919 --> 00:08:29.169
So, if you’re good with numbers, you’re
more likely to be good with language as well.

00:08:29.169 --> 00:08:35.219
Another psychologist, L L Thurston, rejected
Spearman’s idea, suggesting that instead

00:08:35.219 --> 00:08:42.319
of one general intelligence, there were seven
primary mental abilities: spatial ability,

00:08:42.319 --> 00:08:50.110
verbal comprehension, word fluency, perceptual
speed, numerical ability, inductive reasoning,

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:51.850
and memory.

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:57.100
This was where the study of intelligence split
into two broad branches of thought: one which

00:08:57.100 --> 00:09:03.670
sees intelligence as a single, unifying pattern,
and another that sees intelligence as multiple

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:04.850
separate streams.

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:11.120
Spearman’s idea of a single general intelligence
that can be measured and scored in the form

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:16.459
of a number may sound familiar to you. If
you’ve been through a conventional schooling

00:09:16.459 --> 00:09:21.010
system, there’s a good chance you’ll have
done an IQ test before - a series of assessments

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:25.560
designed to establish your Intelligence Quotient.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:31.370
If you have an IQ of 140 or above, you’re
regarded as a genius, and if your IQ is around

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:35.680
100, you have what’s thought of as average
intelligence.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:41.690
For the origins of the IQ test, we need to
go back to 1905, the year after Spearman introduced

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:49.330
his G-factor to the world. In France, two
psychologists, Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon,

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:54.990
devised the Binet-Simon test, which was designed
to identify children who were struggling in

00:09:54.990 --> 00:10:00.040
school and needed to be placed in special
education programs.

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:06.380
Building on the idea of a G-factor, Binet
and Simon’s tests evaluated verbal reasoning,

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:11.939
working memory, and visual-spatial skills
to come up with a mental age which could be

00:10:11.939 --> 00:10:16.189
used to find and help cognitively slower children.

00:10:16.189 --> 00:10:21.370
Although the Binet-Simon test was created
out of a desire to help children who would

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:26.160
otherwise have been sent to mental asylums,
it wouldn’t be long before it was used as

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:30.480
a tool to serve less compassionate ends.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:37.250
In 1912, German psychologist William Stern
built on Binet and Simon’s work, coining

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:43.569
the phrase ‘intelligence quotient’ and
suggesting a new way to calculate it - by

00:10:43.569 --> 00:10:50.500
dividing a person’s mental age by chronological
age and multiplying by 100.

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:52.290
Nothing wrong with that.

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:59.129
But when American psychologist Lewis Terman
revised the Binet-Simon test and began working

00:10:59.129 --> 00:11:05.639
with the American government to change army
selection criteria, things started to go pear-shaped.

00:11:05.639 --> 00:11:11.720
To identify the correct candidates for officer
training, the American military tested 1.75

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:17.519
million men during World War I in the first
mass testing of human intelligence. By this

00:11:17.519 --> 00:11:23.780
time, eugenics - that fun fest of selective
breeding - had grown in popularity. So, the

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:29.430
idea of being able to measure someone’s
value to society with a number fitted like

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:31.930
a hand in a latex glove.

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:37.910
The military testing was problematic for many
reasons, varying from inconsistent assessment

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:43.529
across different military camps to the fact
that tests were based on innate assumptions,

00:11:43.529 --> 00:11:49.290
like the idea intelligence is an inherited
trait. The tests also included questions related

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:55.170
more to American culture than general intelligence.
This placed many of the recruits, specifically

00:11:55.170 --> 00:12:01.069
immigrants to the US, at a disadvantage as
they had little exposure to American society

00:12:01.069 --> 00:12:03.250
and often didn’t speak much English.

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:08.680
As a result, men from minority groups scored
poorly, which was used as evidence to support

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:14.829
the eugenics argument that intelligence was
partly determined by race, and psychologists

00:12:14.829 --> 00:12:22.089
used the data to build a false hierarchy of
ethnic groups based on apparent intellect.

00:12:22.089 --> 00:12:28.740
Soon, this thinking began to influence public
policy too. Henry H Goddard, a psychologist

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:34.800
who had worked with Lewis Terman in bringing
the IQ test to America, was a eugenicist but,

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:41.420
unlike Francis Galton who favoured selective
breeding to enhance positive traits, Goddard

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:45.940
wanted to eliminate undesirable characteristics
too.

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:51.970
He called people who had done poorly in IQ
tests “feeble-minded” and argued that,

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:56.790
because intelligence was hereditary, they
should be prevented from having children.

00:12:56.790 --> 00:13:04.160
And so it was that states like Virginia passed
legislation to allow the forced sterilisation

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:10.350
of people with low IQ scores, with the Supreme
Court upholding the legality of the laws.

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:16.860
By the end of these campaigns, more than 64,000
people had been sterilised in the US based

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:22.249
on their IQ scores, with 20,000 in California
alone.

00:13:22.249 --> 00:13:27.319
The full madness of this approach was made
abundantly clear when the Nazis were so impressed

00:13:27.319 --> 00:13:32.790
by the results in California they came to
ask for advice on the best ways to prevent

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:36.420
the birth of people they deemed unfit.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:41.290
Call me old-fashioned, but if a Nazi arrived
at my door to tell me I was doing a great

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:44.470
job, I’dd probably consider a career change.

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:50.139
Anyway, the Nazis clearly went home brimming
with ideas, because in the years that followed

00:13:50.139 --> 00:13:56.819
they used pseudo IQ tests and other assessments
to justify the sterilisation of more than

00:13:56.819 --> 00:14:04.050
400,000 people, and the murder of another
300,000 who were determined to be ‘life

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:06.910
unworthy of life’.

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:11.949
Back in the US, the horror of what was happening
in Germany led to a rapid decline in the popularity

00:14:11.949 --> 00:14:16.870
of eugenics - there’s nothing like genocide
to take the shine off an ideology, is there?

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:21.840
IQ tests were still considered relevant, but
the way they were used changed. Scientists

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:27.430
began to question the structure of the tests,
proving that cultural and environmental factors

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:29.960
had a significant impact on test results.

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:35.920
They showed, for example, that IQ test scores
improved in successive generations. This was

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:41.809
known as the Flynn Effect, and it was widely
attributed to factors such as better education,

00:14:41.809 --> 00:14:48.009
nutrition, and healthcare. Interestingly enough,
this generation on generation improvement

00:14:48.009 --> 00:14:55.319
in IQ test performance seems to have begun
to reverse over the last couple of decades

00:14:55.319 --> 00:15:01.199
according to a whole raft of different studies
across Europe and Australia. So yeah, it turns

00:15:01.199 --> 00:15:06.029
out people really are getting stupider - something
which should be abundantly clear to anyone

00:15:06.029 --> 00:15:07.870
with an internet connection.

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:12.220
Over the second half of the twentieth century,
it also became increasingly accepted that

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:18.120
IQ tests included unconscious cultural biases
that were skewing test scores. These days

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:22.920
IQ tests are still pretty similar in terms
of questions and design to the original tests,

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:27.740
but we have more sophisticated ways of making
sure they’re free of bias. But they’re

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:33.439
still used in many public systems, like schooling
and the civil service, sometimes in unexpected

00:15:33.439 --> 00:15:40.089
ways. In some areas of the US, for example,
new police recruits may be turned down unless

00:15:40.089 --> 00:15:47.329
they score below a specific IQ, because anyone
scoring higher is expected to get bored.

00:15:47.329 --> 00:15:53.250
Regardless, IQ tests remain controversial
and it's no surprise that psychologists have

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:57.750
continued to search for alternative ways of
measuring intelligence.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:03.350
Perhaps the best-known example is the Theory
of Multiple Intelligences developed by Harvard

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:09.779
professor Howard Gardner as an alternative
to the notion of one general intelligence.

00:16:09.779 --> 00:16:14.940
Echoing the spirit of LL Thurston’s work,
Gardner suggested that we have several different

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:20.999
intelligences that operate fairly independently
of each other. Initially, he pointed to eight

00:16:20.999 --> 00:16:25.019
types:
Verbal-linguistic intelligence, which describes

00:16:25.019 --> 00:16:30.130
your ability with words and language
Logical-mathematical intelligence, which supports

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:34.720
your way with numbers and reasoning
Visual-spatial intelligence, which relates

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:37.879
to how you understand maps and other visual
stuff

00:16:37.879 --> 00:16:42.399
Musical intelligence, which is about, well,
music

00:16:42.399 --> 00:16:47.291
Naturalistic intelligence, which is how well
you can comprehend the natural world, like

00:16:47.291 --> 00:16:52.970
different plant species and the weather
Bodily-kinesthetic intelligence is the ability

00:16:52.970 --> 00:16:58.510
to use your body intelligently - for a real-world
example of this, watch out for an upcoming

00:16:58.510 --> 00:17:03.249
video in which I will communicate the entire
message through interpretive dance

00:17:03.249 --> 00:17:08.480
Interpersonal intelligence describes how well
you read and engage with other people; and

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:12.049
Intrapersonal intelligence has to do with
your capacity for self-awareness

00:17:12.049 --> 00:17:18.309
Later, Gardner also added Existential intelligence,
which centres on your ability to think about

00:17:18.309 --> 00:17:23.679
and answer the more profound questions about,
like, life and stuff.

00:17:23.679 --> 00:17:29.140
Intuitively, the idea of multiple intelligences
makes sense. It helps explain how a person

00:17:29.140 --> 00:17:35.070
can be a musical ignoramus but have the navigational
abilities of a homing pigeon, or why my mate

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:39.419
Dave got arrested for indecency despite being
pretty good at maths.

00:17:39.419 --> 00:17:43.230
This approach also fits well with some other
types of intelligence, like the emotional

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:48.550
intelligence theory first developed by Peter
Salovey and John Mayer, then later popularised

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:50.020
by Daniel Goleman.

00:17:50.020 --> 00:17:54.600
Gardner’s theory of multiple intelligences
has gained massive popularity in education

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:59.910
systems around the world and has been applied
in various industries, but critics say it

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:02.640
lacks the scientific evidence to back it up.

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:08.121
As another alternative, psychologist Robert
Sternberg came up with the triarchic theory

00:18:08.121 --> 00:18:13.590
of intelligence. Sternberg agreed with Gardner
that there’s more than one type of intelligence,

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:19.550
but limited his selection to three: analytical
intelligence, or problem-solving ability,

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:23.010
creative intelligence, and practical intelligence.

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:28.140
He also argued that these types of intelligence
contributed to real-world success, but criticisms

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:31.960
of his theory point to the fact that there
appears to be a correlation between these

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:37.090
different intelligences, so if you’re high
in one you’re likely to be high in the others.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:43.970
That, of course, takes us back to the start
and the notion of a single general intelligence,

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:45.090
G.

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:49.620
So, if you’re ever worried that you’re
not actually as smart as you think you are,

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:53.960
don’t fret. Nobody seems to have the faintest
idea what intelligence is anyway, so you have

00:18:53.960 --> 00:18:58.600
a whole bunch of different versions to choose
from, and you’re bound to be a genius in

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:04.230
one of them.At the very least, you’ve proved
you’re smart enough to watch brain-stretching

00:19:04.230 --> 00:19:16.119
youtube videos, and that’s got to count
for something.

